{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1330 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "f17e142b08c61a2f7b03cda481f1f4806df9c7b8dbdc00736cf46d144faf5c72" :edb/db-before-t 1329 :edb/db-after-t 1330 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534642 50 #inst "2026-10-08T03:04:14.451Z" 13194139534642 true] [17592186126868 1104 #edb/ref 17592186126870 13194139534642 true] [17592186126871 1104 #edb/ref 17592186126874 13194139534642 true] [17592186126875 1104 #edb/ref 17592186126877 13194139534642 true] [17592186128819 1100 "Find the fourth term of the sequence given by the recurrence relation\n\n$$\na_n = 5a_{n-1}+a_{n-2},\n\\qquad\na_1 = 2, \n\\quad\na_2 = 1, \n\\quad\nn \\geq 3.\n$$" 13194139534642 true] [17592186128819 1100 "Find the fourth term of the sequence given by the recurrence relation\n$a_n = 5a_{n-1}+a_{n-2}, \\qquad a_1 = 2, \\quad a_2 = 1, \\quad n \\geq 3$." 13194139534642 false] [17592186128819 1145 "Starting with the initial terms $a_1 = 2$, and $a_2 = 1$, we compute the next terms using the recursive formula until we reach the fourth term.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{1} &= 2 \\\\\na_{2} &= 1 \\\\\na_{3} &= 5a_{2} + a_{1} = 5 \\cdot 1 + 2 = 5 + 2 = 7 \\\\\na_{4} &= 5a_{3} + a_{2} = 5 \\cdot 7 + 1 = 35 + 1 = 36\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fourth term is $a_4 = 36$." 13194139534642 false] [17592186128819 1145 "Starting with the initial terms $a_1 = 2,$ and $a_2 = 1,$ we compute the next terms using the recursive formula until we reach the fourth term.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =2 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =5{a}_{2}+{a}_{1}=5\\cdot 1+2=5+2=7 \\\\ {a}_{4} & =5{a}_{3}+{a}_{2}=5\\cdot 7+1=35+1=36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fourth term is $a_4 = 36.$" 13194139534642 true] [17592186128820 1100 "Which of the following linear recurrence relations are homogeneous?\n\n- $a_n = a_{n-1} + 5a_{n-2}+n$\n\n- $b_n = 3b_{n-1} -2b_{n-2}$\n\n- $c_n = 2c_{n-1} + 3c_{n-2}- c_{n-3}$" 13194139534642 true] [17592186128820 1100 "Which of the following linear recurrence relations are homogeneous?\n\n1. $a_n = a_{n-1} + 5a_{n-2}+n$\n2. $b_n = 3b_{n-1} -2b_{n-2}$\n3. $c_n = 2c_{n-1} + 3c_{n-2}- c_{n-3}$" 13194139534642 false] [17592186128820 1145 "A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is **homogeneous** if the forcing function is zero.\n\nIn other words, the linear recurrence relation\n\n$$\na_n = s_1a_{n-1} + s_2a_{n-2} + \\ldots + s_ka_{n-k} + g(n)\n$$\n\nis homogeneous if $g(n) = 0$ for all $n$, and $s_i$ is constant for each $i$.\n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- $a_n$ is not homogeneous. The term $n$ of the expression doesn't contain preceding terms of the relation.\n- $b_n$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, $b_{n-1}$ and $b_{n-2}$.\n- $c_n$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, $c_{n-1}$, $c_{n-2}$, and $c_{n-3}$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534642 false] [17592186128820 1145 "A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is **homogeneous** if the forcing function is zero. \n\nIn other words, the linear recurrence relation\n\n$$\na_n = s_1a_{n-1} + s_2a_{n-2} + \\ldots + s_ka_{n-k} + g(n),\n$$\n\nis homogeneous if $g(n) = 0$ for all $n,$ and $s_i$ is constant for each $i.$\n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- $a_n$ is not homogeneous. The term $n$ of the expression doesn't contain preceding terms of the relation.\n\n- $b_n$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, $b_{n-1}$ and $b_{n-2}.$\n\n- $c_n$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, $c_{n-1},$ $c_{n-2},$ and $c_{n-3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534642 true] [17592186128821 1100 "Fully classify the following recurrence relation:\n\n$$\na_n =  a_{n-2} - \\dfrac23 a_{n-1},\n\\qquad\na_1 =0, \n\\quad\na_2 = 1,\n\\quad\nn \\geq 2.\n$$" 13194139534642 true] [17592186128821 1100 "Fully classify the following recurrence relation:\n$a_n = a_{n-2} - \\dfrac23 a_{n-1}, \\qquad a_1 =0, \\quad a_2 = 1, \\quad n \\geq 2$." 13194139534642 false] [17592186128821 1145 "Recall the following classifications of recurrence relations:\n\n- The order of a relation $a_n$ is the largest index difference between $a_n$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\n- A recurrence relation is *linear (with constant coefficients)* if it expresses $a_n$ as a sum of previous terms $a_{n-1}, \\dots, a_{n-k}$ with constant coefficients, together with possibly a final term that depends only on $n{:}$\n\n$$\na_n = s_{1}a_{n-1} + s_{2}a_{n-2} + \\ldots + s_{k}a_{n-k} + g(n)\n$$\n\nwhere $s_1, s_2, \\ldots, s_k$ are constants and $g(n)$ is a function, often called a *forcing function*, that does not involve $a_n$ or its preceding terms.\n\n- A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is *homogeneous* if the forcing function is zero. Otherwise, it is *inhomogeneous.*\n\nLet's classify the relation $a_n =  a_{n-2} - \\dfrac23 a_{n-1}.$\n\n- The largest index difference between $a_n$ and the preceding terms $a_{n-1}$ and $a_{n-2}$ in the relation is $2.$ So, the relation has order $2.$\n\n- The previous terms $a_{n-1}$ and $a_{n-2}$ occur only in the first power and their  coefficients are  constants. So, the relation is linear.\n\n- The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, $a_{n-1}$ and $a_{n-2}.$ So, the relation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the recurrence relation $a_n$ has order\n$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2},$\nis\n$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{linear}},$\nand is\n$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{homogeneous}}.$" 13194139534642 true] [17592186128821 1145 "Recall the following classifications of recurrence relations:\n\n- The order of a relation $a_n$ is the largest index difference between $a_n$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n- A recurrence relation is *linear (with constant coefficients)* if it expresses $a_n$ as a sum of previous terms $a_{n-1}, \\dots, a_{n-k}$ with constant coefficients, together with possibly a final term that depends only on $n{:}$\n$a_n = s_{1}a_{n-1} + s_{2}a_{n-2} + \\ldots + s_{k}a_{n-k} + g(n)$\nwhere $s_1, s_2, \\ldots, s_k$ are constants and $g(n)$ is a function, often called a *forcing function*, that does not involve $a_n$ or its preceding terms.\n- A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is *homogeneous* if the forcing function is zero. Otherwise, it is *inhomogeneous.*\n\nLet's classify the relation\n\n$$\na_n = a_{n-2} - \\dfrac23 a_{n-1}\n$$\n\n- The largest index difference between $a_n$ and the preceding terms $a_{n-1}$ and $a_{n-2}$ in the relation is $2$. So, the relation has order $2$.\n- The previous terms $a_{n-1}$ and $a_{n-2}$ occur only in the first power and their coefficients are constants. So, the relation is linear.\n- The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, $a_{n-1}$ and $a_{n-2}$. So, the relation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the recurrence relation $a_n$ has order\n$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}$,\nis\n$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{linear}}$,\nand is\n$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{homogeneous}}$." 13194139534642 false] [17592186129221 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300724 13194139534642 true] [17592186129221 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300731 13194139534642 true] [17592186129221 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300766 13194139534642 true] [17592186129222 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300717 13194139534642 true] [17592186129222 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300773 13194139534642 true] [17592186129222 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300780 13194139534642 true] [17592186129222 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300787 13194139534642 true] [17592186129223 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300744 13194139534642 true] [17592186129223 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300755 13194139534642 true] [17592186129223 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300794 13194139534642 true] [17592186129223 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300802 13194139534642 true] [17592186129508 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534642 true] [17592186129508 1100 "Find the fifth term of the sequence given by the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=2{a}_{n-1}-3,\\,{a}_{1}=5,\\,n\\ge 2.\n$$" 13194139534642 true] [17592186129508 1100 "Find the fifth term of the sequence given by the recurrence relation\n\n$a_{n} = 2a_{n - 1} - 3, a_{1} = 5, n \\ge 2$." 13194139534642 false] [17592186129508 1145 "Starting with the first term ${a}_{1}=5,$ we compute the next terms using the recursive formula until we reach the fifth term.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =5 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =2{a}_{1}-3=2\\cdot 5-3=7 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =2{a}_{2}-3=2\\cdot 7-3=11 \\\\ {a}_{4} & =2{a}_{3}-3=2\\cdot 11-3=19 \\\\ {a}_{5} & =2{a}_{4}-3=2\\cdot 19-3=35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fifth term is ${a}_{5}=35.$" 13194139534642 true] [17592186129510 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534642 true] [17592186129510 1100 "Which of the following recurrence relations are first-order?\n\n- ${a}_{n}=2{a}_{n-2}$\n- ${b}_{n}={b}_{n-1}^{2}+2{b}_{n-2}$\n- ${c}_{n}=2{c}_{n-1}+n$" 13194139534642 true] [17592186129510 1100 "Which of the following recurrence relations are first-order?\n\n1. $a_{n} = 2a_{n - 2}$\n2. $b_{n} = b_{n - 1}^{2} + 2b_{n - 2}$\n3. $c_{n} = 2c_{n - 1} + n$" 13194139534642 false] [17592186129510 1145 "The order of a recurrence relation is the largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- The largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding term ${a}_{n-2}$ in the relation is $2.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $2.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${b}_{n}$ and the preceding terms ${b}_{n-1}$ and ${b}_{n-2}$ in the relation is $2.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $2.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${c}_{n}$ and the preceding term ${c}_{n-1}$ in the relation is $1.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534642 true] [17592186129511 1017 #edb/ref 17592186126852 13194139534642 false] [17592186129511 1017 #edb/ref 17592186300738 13194139534642 true] [17592186129511 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534642 true] [17592186129511 1100 "Find the fourth term of the sequence given by the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=3{a}_{n-1}+2{a}_{n-2},\\,{a}_{1}=2,\\,{a}_{2}=4,\\,n\\ge 3.\n$$" 13194139534642 true] [17592186129511 1100 "Find the fourth term of the sequence given by the recurrence relation\n\n$a_{n} = 3a_{n - 1} + 2a_{n - 2}, a_{1} = 2, a_{2} = 4, n \\ge 3$." 13194139534642 false] [17592186129511 1145 "Starting with the initial terms ${a}_{1}=2,$ and ${a}_{2}=4,$ we compute the next terms using the recursive formula until we reach the fourth term.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =2 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =4 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =3{a}_{2}+2{a}_{1}=3\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 2=12+4=16 \\\\ {a}_{4} & =3{a}_{3}+2{a}_{2}=3\\cdot 16+2\\cdot 4=48+8=56\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fourth term is ${a}_{4}=56.$" 13194139534642 true] [17592186129513 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534642 true] [17592186129513 1100 "Classify the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=2{a}_{n-1}-3{n}^{2},\\,{a}_{1}=10,\\,n\\ge 2.\n$$\n\nThe recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n{{field-1}},\nis\n{{field-2}},\nand is\n{{field-3}}." 13194139534642 true] [17592186129513 1100 "Classify the recurrence relation\n$a_{n} = 2a_{n - 1} - 3n^{2}, a_{1} = 10, n \\ge 2$.\nThe recurrence relation $a_{n}$ has order\n{{field-1}},\nis\n{{field-2}},\nand is\n{{field-3}}." 13194139534642 false] [17592186129513 1145 "Recall the following classifications of recurrence relations:\n\n- The order of a relation ${a}_{n}$ is the largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\n- A recurrence relation is *linear (with constant coefficients)* if it expresses ${a}_{n}$ as a sum of previous terms ${a}_{n-1},…,{a}_{n-k}$ with constant coefficients, together with possibly a final term that depends only on $n:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n)\n$$\n\nwhere ${s}_{1},{s}_{2},…,{s}_{k}$ are constants and $g(n)$ is a function, often called a *forcing function*, that does not involve ${a}_{n}$ or its preceding terms.\n\n- A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is *homogeneous* if the forcing function is zero. Otherwise, it is *inhomogeneous.*\n\nLet's classify the relation ${a}_{n}=2{a}_{n-1}-3{n}^{2}.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding term ${a}_{n-1}$ in the relation is $1.$ So, the relation has order $1.$\n\n- The previous term ${a}_{n-1}$ appears in the first power with constant  coefficient $2.$ So, the relation is linear.\n\n- The relation is linear, and the forcing function $g(n)=-3{n}^{2}$ is nonzero. So, the relation is inhomogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n$1,$\nis\n$\\text{linear},$\nand is\n$\\text{inhomogeneous}.$" 13194139534642 true] [17592186300717 1017 #edb/ref 17592186300718 13194139534642 true] [17592186300717 1089 "q-253144" 13194139534642 true] [17592186300717 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534642 true] [17592186300717 1100 "Which of the following linear recurrence relations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${a}_{n}=2{a}_{n-1}+5{a}_{n-2}$\n- ${b}_{n}={b}_{n-1}+3{b}_{n-2}$\n- ${c}_{n}=4{c}_{n-1}+7n$" 13194139534642 true] [17592186300717 1113 #uuid "6af1f1dd-0830-5336-97d9-6d826a433e28" 13194139534642 true] [17592186300717 1145 "A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is **homogeneous** if the forcing function is zero. \n\nIn other words, the linear recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n),\n$$\n\nis homogeneous if $g(n)=0$ for all $n,$ and ${s}_{i}$ is constant for each $i.$\n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- ${a}_{n}$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, ${a}_{n-1}$ and ${a}_{n-2}.$\n\n- ${b}_{n}$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, ${b}_{n-1}$ and ${b}_{n-2}.$\n\n- ${c}_{n}$ is not homogeneous. The term $7n$ of the expression doesn't contain preceding terms of the relation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534642 true] [17592186300718 1000 #edb/ref 17592186300719 13194139534642 true] [17592186300718 1000 #edb/ref 17592186300720 13194139534642 true] [17592186300718 1000 #edb/ref 17592186300721 13194139534642 true] [17592186300718 1000 #edb/ref 17592186300722 13194139534642 true] [17592186300718 1000 #edb/ref 17592186300723 13194139534642 true] [17592186300718 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534642 true] [17592186300718 1041 "selection" 13194139534642 true] [17592186300718 1104 #edb/ref 17592186300722 13194139534642 true] [17592186300718 1110 #uuid "040ccf82-071a-5447-bab7-f9a303883c5a" 13194139534642 true] [17592186300719 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534642 true] [17592186300719 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534642 true] [17592186300719 1111 #uuid "acfd1a4b-8a3a-578c-9b26-541bc83beeb4" 13194139534642 true] [17592186300720 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534642 true] [17592186300720 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534642 true] [17592186300720 1111 #uuid "bf61f159-fabf-5cb6-8bcf-f4d03c42ef03" 13194139534642 true] [17592186300721 1034 "I only" 13194139534642 true] [17592186300721 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534642 true] [17592186300721 1111 #uuid "873f2c05-12cf-5210-962f-d1c4ebe188a9" 13194139534642 true] [17592186300722 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534642 true] [17592186300722 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534642 true] [17592186300722 1111 #uuid "006c188d-38c8-5e26-a134-a13fb7cefd5c" 13194139534642 true] [17592186300723 1034 "II only" 13194139534642 true] [17592186300723 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534642 true] [17592186300723 1111 #uuid "04117c07-cab8-5239-966a-b01e9b7ec735" 13194139534642 true] [17592186300724 1017 #edb/ref